Off-gassing is what a change keeps releasing after the visible event is over. You can walk out of a hard conversation and feel your shoulders drop in the parking lot. You find the words you wanted that night. A week later you realize you’ve stopped rehearsing what you’d say if it happened again. One event, three layers letting go on different clocks.

Layer	What can be released	How it might show up
Body	Physical tension	A long exhale, unclenching your jaw, shaking out your hands
Emotion	Feeling you were holding back to get through the moment	Tears after the work is done, laughter when the tension breaks
Language	Something you couldn’t say while you were in it	The sentence comes to you later, or you write it somewhere private
Memory	An old interpretation of what happened	You remember the same scene and realize it means something different to you now
Relationship	A role you’ve been keeping up between you	You stop smoothing over every disagreement. The other person starts bringing things up directly
Group	A concern people were each carrying privately	One person says it out loud and several others finally give their own accounts

And one layer’s release can become the next layer’s trigger. Your laugh releases your tension. Someone else hears it as permission to speak. What they say changes how you remember the whole exchange. Our Dragonfly can follow that sequence and see where the release eased pressure, where it moved pressure somewhere else, and where it gave somebody a new move.

The Silent Guitar version is especially subtle. A release can show up as a missing repetition. You tell the old story and leave out the sentence you used to tack on to justify yourself. Nobody else hears a dramatic ending. You hear the rest because you know where that note used to be.

That’s the link to knowledge as a memory virus. An idea can keep working in the background and surface later as a series of small releases. To see which layer changed, watch what happens the next time the old cue comes around.